Dual-use disclosure for @scaffoldly/rowdy-vfs

What this package contains

  lib/linux-x64/vfspreload.so and lib/linux-arm64/vfspreload.so are small
  compiled shared objects (ELF, built against glibc 2.36; loaded by musl
  and glibc). The same two files are also inlined into dist/index.js as
  base64 so a bundled consumer can write them to disk at runtime
  (materialize()). They are built in GitHub Actions from
  native/vfspreload.c (which includes native/vfs_core.h,
  native/vfs_transport.h and native/vfs_fault.h) on the `vfs` branch of https://github.com/scaffoldly/rowdy
  and the published tarball carries sigstore provenance for that build.

What the shared object does

  It is an LD_PRELOAD interposer for libc path functions (open, stat, access,
  opendir, mkdir, unlink, rename, link, symlink, chdir, getcwd, realpath,
  readlink, exec*, close/fsync for files it opened, and the single-path
  syscalls via syscall(2)). When a path starts with VFS_PREFIX (default /vfs)
  it is rewritten to VFS_BACKING (default /tmp/vfsstore) before the real libc
  function is called. Paths returned to the program (getcwd, realpath,
  readlink) are rewritten back. The program reads and writes ordinary files
  under VFS_BACKING through the unmodified libc data path. VFS_MOUNTS
  ("prefix=backing" entries separated by ':') declares several such
  prefix/directory pairs at once; a path is rewritten by the longest prefix
  it falls under.

Local supervisor socket

  When VFS_SOCKET is set (rowdy sets it to /tmp/rowdy/vfs.sock), the shim
  connects to that unix-domain stream socket, which is created and owned by
  the supervisor process that launched the program (rowdy). It is used to
  ask the supervisor to populate a mount's backing directory before a path is
  used and to persist it afterwards. The shim never opens TCP/IP sockets, never resolves
  DNS, and never talks to anything other than that one local socket. When
  VFS_SOCKET is unset or the socket is absent, no connection is made and the
  shim is a plain local-directory overlay.

  Protocol: 9P2000.L, the Linux dialect of the Plan 9 file protocol, as a
  client. Messages are size[4] type[1] tag[2] followed by the message's
  fields; errors come back as Rlerror carrying a Linux errno. The shim sends
  only the control messages, never Tread or Twrite: file contents stay on the
  local backing directory and go through the unmodified libc data path.

    Message                                  When the shim sends it
    Tversion, Tattach(aname=prefix)          on connect, once per mount
    Twalk(names) + Tclunk                    before stat/access/open on a path
    Twalk + Tlopen(O_RDONLY) + Tclunk        before open(P) for reading
    Twalk + Tlopen(O_DIRECTORY) + Tclunk     before opendir/scandir(P)
    Twalk + Tlopen(flags)                    after open(P) for writing; the fid
                                             stays with the descriptor
    Tfsync(fid)                              on fsync of a written descriptor
    Tclunk(fid)                              on the last close of a written
                                             descriptor (the supervisor persists)
    Twalk + Tlopen(O_WRONLY) + Tclunk        after truncate/link/symlink on P
    Twalk(parent) + Tmkdir(name)             after mkdir(P)
    Twalk(parent) + Tunlinkat(name)          after unlink/rmdir(P)
    Twalk(parents) + Trenameat               after rename(A, B)
    Tlock(fid, RDLCK)                        when the program takes a read lock
                                             (fcntl F_RDLCK / SQLite SHARED)
    Tlock(fid, WRLCK)                        when the program takes a write lock
                                             (fcntl F_WRLCK / SQLite RESERVED)
    Tlock(fid, UNLCK)                        when the program drops a write lock
                                             (fcntl F_UNLCK)

  The lock messages exist so that a supervisor backed by shared storage can
  serialize writers across machines (a lease held for the duration of a write
  transaction). The shim learns about them by interposing fcntl(2) byte-range
  locking on descriptors it opened under a mount; it still performs the real
  fcntl locally. A supervisor that answers Tlock with status BLOCKED makes
  fcntl return EAGAIN, exactly as a contended POSIX lock would, so programs
  such as SQLite retry under their own busy-timeout. Nothing else about
  locking is changed or hidden from the program.

  Paths cross the socket as walk names relative to a mount. The supervisor
  decides what backs them (a local directory, or an object store such as S3
  using the supervisor's own credentials). The shim holds no credentials of
  any kind.

Fault report

  When the program dies of a SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, SIGILL or SIGFPE raised by the
  kernel, the shim writes a description of the fault to the program's own
  stderr: the signal, the faulting address, the shim's own commit, the
  program counter, the stack pointer, every general register, the code bytes
  around the program counter, return addresses found by frame pointer and by
  scanning the 16 KiB above the stack pointer for code addresses (each with
  the /proc/self/maps line it falls in), and the libc function (and path)
  the faulting thread and every other thread was inside, if any, with the 9P
  message each was waiting on. It reads /proc/self/maps and the program's
  own readable memory to do so, nothing else; it sends nothing anywhere. The
  program then dies by the same signal as it would have without the shim.

  For this the shim keeps its own handler for those four signals: it
  interposes sigaction(2) and signal(3) for them only, records
  the program's handler instead of installing it, and calls that handler from
  its own (node's, which handles WebAssembly memory traps, is called first
  through v8::TryHandleWebAssemblyTrapPosix). It interposes pthread_create(3)
  to give each new thread a 64 KiB alternate signal stack, so a thread that
  overflowed its stack can still be reported, and keeps a list of live
  threads so the report can say what each was doing. A signal sent by
  kill(2) is passed to the program unreported, as is a fault in a program
  whose own handler (other than node's SIGSEGV handler) may recover from it.

  All of this is off unless VFS_FAULT_REPORT=1 (rowdy sets it when its own
  log level is debug or trace). Off, no handler is installed, no thread gets
  an alternate stack, and the sigaction, signal and pthread_create wrappers
  pass every call straight to libc.

Why it exists

  rowdy (https://github.com/scaffoldly/rowdy) is an entrypoint supervisor for
  containers running on AWS Lambda. The Lambda sandbox denies every
  kernel-mediated way to present a virtual filesystem (no /dev/fuse,
  mount(2), unshare, ptrace and seccomp-notify all return EPERM), so the only
  way to give an application a writable, persistent directory tree is to
  rewrite its libc path calls in-process. rowdy prepends this shim to the
  LD_PRELOAD of the application it launches, and only when the operator opts
  in with ROWDY_VFS=1.

What it does not do

  - No TCP/IP, no DNS, no connections other than the local VFS_SOCKET above.
  - No reads or writes outside VFS_BACKING beyond passing calls through to
    the real libc unchanged.
  - No credential, environment or keystroke capture; no process injection
    beyond the LD_PRELOAD the launching process sets explicitly.
  - No install-time scripts in this package; nothing runs on npm install.
  - Does not load itself into any process unless LD_PRELOAD names it.

Intended legitimate use

  Giving a container application a writable virtual directory, backed by a
  local directory or by object storage, under a serverless runtime that
  forbids mounts. Enabled per deployment by the operator who owns both the
  container and the function.

